</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We track three categories of metrics. For <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">efficiency</span>, we report end-to-end inference time and speedup relative to Original. For <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">quality</span>, we use VBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> total score and its 16 sub-dimensions, and we additionally record LPIPS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, SSIM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2004</a>)</cite>, and PSNR as supplementary diagnostics. For <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">memory</span>, we report peak GPU memory during inference.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Baselines</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our main comparison includes the following training-free baselines. <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Original</span> is the unaccelerated inference pipeline. <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-DiT</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024b</a>)</cite> performs block-level reuse adapted to the target AR pipelines by applying block-skip decisions per AR unit rather than globally. <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TeaCache</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> performs binary timestep-aware caching based on step similarity estimation. <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TaylorSeer</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025b</a>)</cite> is a forecast-style accelerator. <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FlowCache</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2026</a>)</cite> applies chunk-aware adaptive caching for autoregressive video generation. <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SCOPE</span> is our predictive caching method with selective computation. Detailed per-method hyperparameter settings are listed in the supplementary material.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Implementation Details</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The default SCOPE configuration uses a cache threshold <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> and a prediction threshold <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> to partition steps into <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Cache</span>, <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Predict</span>, and <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Recompute</span>. On SkyReels-V2, the paper-core configuration uses <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{c}=0.18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.18</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{c}=0.18</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{p}=0.42" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.42</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{p}=0.42</annotation></semantics></math>, second-order Taylor prediction, prediction decay 0.75, bounded skip length 5, and selective computation enabled. On MAGI-1, <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{c}=0.03" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.03</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{c}=0.03</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{p}=0.08" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.08</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{p}=0.08</annotation></semantics></math>, second-order Taylor prediction, prediction decay 0.75, and bounded skip length 5. These paper-core settings are chosen as practical operating points on the speed-quality frontier rather than to optimize a single metric. The sensitivity analysis in subsection <a href="#S4.SS6" title="4.6. Sensitivity Analysis ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Not All Frames Deserve Full Computation: Accelerating Autoregressive Video Generation via Selective Computation and Predictive Extrapolation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a> shows the resulting tradeoff is smooth and the method is robust within a reasonable range. For each model, method, and configuration, we generate over 400 videos to ensure statistically meaningful comparisons. FlashAttention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dao et al., 2022</a>)</cite> is enabled by default for all experiments. All experiments are conducted on a single NVIDIA A800 80GB GPU under the same inference setting.</p>
</div>
